
On the 25th a new space was opened with Copilot. We began by examining the floor.py script to expand the floor generator functions started on day 1. With Copilot a Blender add-on was created which generates hexagonal tilings that could be used for floor shapes. I spent a long time trying to figure out how to get the add-on into Blender which was installed in a protected folder. I re-downloaded a standalone version of Blender and installed the add-ons into that. The generator was a great tool for accelerating level production. We added new features to the add-on, like the ability to segment the hexagons into triangles and add offset between them.

Following that we explored different options for storing the generated floorplan. Scripts were developed for placing generated geometry into OBJ files, thus bypassing Blender and adding compatability, and taking advantage of GitHub OBJ viewing functions. Another script was created to parse a text file holding a list of commands for level generation and executing those commands.

Working with Copilot produced very good and usable results, but there are serious issues to be worked through. The biggest problem was poor planning. I approached the interaction as an experiment and, for the most part, made it up as I went along. This produced a lot of excess code. Another related problem was keeping up with Copilot. The code produced is very formal and difficult for me to understand, and therefore counterproductive when code-editing is required. And because I didn't come up with the approach for the code layout, I had to spend time trying to figure it out. There is a large amount of code to sort through and organize, and that's going to take a fair amount of time.

I spent the rest of the session re-thinking how to approach working with Copilot. I used some of the created tools to generate geometry for level 1, the virtual facility, then spent some time copying generated parts and developed the level manually.

I decided that I first should go through all of the scripts that were created and add comments line by line so that I can understand how they function. Then I need to decide which of the above processes should be used so that I can organize the code to fit my process and discard the remainder, or maybe I'll put it in a miscellaneous repository. I'm also considering changing the way I interact with Copilot. It might be a better approach for me to create an outline, or come up with an outline alongside Copilot, then create a file of empty functions and fill in one function at a time. That way I can understand how the code is wired together as a whole, and understand how each function works individually.
